<!--
LAYOUT LAW (summary — the full constitution is FIGURE_CONSTITUTION.md):

1.  Prose first, figure second. Every figure is introduced by a lead-in
    sentence in the preceding paragraph that names the question it answers.
    A figure never opens a section; a reader should meet the idea before
    the control.
2.  One archetype per concept. Two figures in one essay may not share an
    interaction pattern — that is what makes each one expressive (Article 1).
3.  Interpretation after the figure. One or two sentences that read the
    figure's *default* state aloud, so the static page (curl, no JS,
    screen reader) still makes the point the figure makes.
4.  Figures carry no facts the prose does not also carry. The figure is a
    way of seeing the essay's own numbers (Article 2), never a second essay.
5.  Spacing: figures flow in the prose column (`my-8` chrome), never side
    by side, never inside KeyTakeaways or Callouts.
-->

## The problem in one paragraph

Answer-first prose. The first paragraph of the body is 40–60 words and
states the finding, not the itinerary (check:site enforces this).

## Section one — the concept

Lead-in sentence naming the question, then the figure:

<__FIGURE_1__ />

Read the default state aloud here: what the reader is looking at before
they touch anything, and what changes when they do.

## Section two — the mechanism

A different archetype for a different kind of understanding:

<__FIGURE_2__ />

Interpretation: the sentence that survives the figure being scrolled past.

## Section three — the evidence

Tables and result charts carry evidence; interactive figures carry
mechanisms. Keep the two apart.

